WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.789
In September of nineteen seventy seven, a

00:00:02.789 --> 00:00:04.514
machine about the size of a small

00:00:04.514 --> 00:00:06.048
car was bolted to the top of

00:00:06.048 --> 00:00:08.540
a rocket and pointed away from everything

00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:09.709
it had ever known.

00:00:10.229 --> 00:00:11.327
It had one job.

00:00:11.327 --> 00:00:13.931
Fly past the outer planets, take pictures,

00:00:13.931 --> 00:00:15.028
and send them home.

00:00:15.549 --> 00:00:17.022
It was never coming back.

00:00:17.022 --> 00:00:18.495
The people who built it knew that.

00:00:18.495 --> 00:00:20.331
They packed it a lunch anyway.

00:00:20.851 --> 00:00:23.052
Bolted to its side, a gold plated

00:00:23.052 --> 00:00:23.767
record.

00:00:23.767 --> 00:00:26.095
Greetings in fifty five languages.

00:00:26.095 --> 00:00:26.810
Whale song.

00:00:26.810 --> 00:00:29.368
Photographs of the world it was leaving.

00:00:29.368 --> 00:00:32.388
And instructions, etched into the cover, for

00:00:32.388 --> 00:00:34.485
anyone who might one day find it.

00:00:35.005 --> 00:00:36.093
Then they let it go.

00:00:36.614 --> 00:00:38.781
For eighteen months it fell outward through

00:00:38.781 --> 00:00:40.972
the dark, a small bright point, talking

00:00:40.972 --> 00:00:41.959
the whole way.

00:00:42.479 --> 00:00:45.125
In March of nineteen seventy nine, Jupiter

00:00:45.125 --> 00:00:46.511
filled its cameras.

00:00:46.511 --> 00:00:48.376
A storm wider than the Earth, slowly

00:00:48.376 --> 00:00:48.982
turning.

00:00:49.501 --> 00:00:51.046
It stole a little speed from the

00:00:51.046 --> 00:00:52.393
giant as it went past.

00:00:52.393 --> 00:00:54.181
The giant never noticed.

00:00:54.701 --> 00:00:56.509
Twenty months later, Saturn.

00:00:56.509 --> 00:00:57.972
It swung in close to the moon

00:00:57.972 --> 00:01:00.178
Titan to look for an atmosphere, and

00:01:00.178 --> 00:01:00.826
found one.

00:01:01.346 --> 00:01:02.940
That choice cost it the rest of

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:03.646
the tour.

00:01:03.646 --> 00:01:05.467
Titan bent its path up and out

00:01:05.467 --> 00:01:07.516
of the flat plane the planets travel

00:01:07.516 --> 00:01:07.994
in.

00:01:08.514 --> 00:01:10.161
There was nothing ahead of it any

00:01:10.161 --> 00:01:10.506
more.

00:01:10.506 --> 00:01:11.511
Nothing but distance.

00:01:12.031 --> 00:01:14.202
Thirteen years after launch, out past the

00:01:14.202 --> 00:01:16.601
orbit of Neptune, it was asked to

00:01:16.601 --> 00:01:18.216
turn around one last time.

00:01:18.735 --> 00:01:20.592
It aimed its cameras back the way

00:01:20.592 --> 00:01:22.448
it had come and took sixty photographs

00:01:22.448 --> 00:01:23.024
of home.

00:01:23.544 --> 00:01:25.547
In one of them, caught by accident

00:01:25.547 --> 00:01:27.340
in a stray beam of sunlight, there

00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:29.038
is a single pale blue pixel.

00:01:29.558 --> 00:01:30.685
That is everyone.

00:01:30.685 --> 00:01:33.661
Every war, every love letter, every person

00:01:33.661 --> 00:01:35.426
who has ever lived, on one speck

00:01:35.426 --> 00:01:37.293
of dust suspended in a sunbeam.

00:01:37.813 --> 00:01:39.672
Then the cameras went off for good,

00:01:39.672 --> 00:01:41.550
to save power, and it kept going.

00:01:42.070 --> 00:01:44.088
On the twenty fifth of August, twenty

00:01:44.088 --> 00:01:46.316
twelve, the particles around it changed.

00:01:46.837 --> 00:01:48.671
The solar wind, the thin breath our

00:01:48.671 --> 00:01:50.601
sun has been exhaling for four and

00:01:50.601 --> 00:01:52.884
a half billion years, simply stopped.

00:01:53.404 --> 00:01:54.778
It had crossed the edge.

00:01:54.778 --> 00:01:56.326
The first thing we ever made to

00:01:56.326 --> 00:01:58.281
leave the sun behind entirely.

00:01:58.801 --> 00:02:00.018
It is still out there.

00:02:00.018 --> 00:02:00.836
Still talking.

00:02:01.356 --> 00:02:03.441
Its voice is twenty two watts, about

00:02:03.441 --> 00:02:04.969
the same as the light in a

00:02:04.969 --> 00:02:05.427
fridge.

00:02:05.427 --> 00:02:07.392
By the time that whisper reaches the

00:02:07.392 --> 00:02:09.694
dishes in the desert, it is fainter

00:02:09.694 --> 00:02:11.877
than almost anything we know how to

00:02:11.877 --> 00:02:12.336
hear.

00:02:12.855 --> 00:02:15.160
Every year it gets a little colder.

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:17.028
Instruments shut down one by one to

00:02:17.028 --> 00:02:18.502
keep the radio alive.

00:02:19.023 --> 00:02:20.836
Some time in the thirties, the last

00:02:20.836 --> 00:02:22.437
of them will go quiet, and it

00:02:22.437 --> 00:02:23.730
will stop answering.

00:02:24.250 --> 00:02:26.027
And then it will simply keep going,

00:02:26.027 --> 00:02:28.126
in the dark, carrying its record, for

00:02:28.126 --> 00:02:29.394
a very long time.

00:02:29.914 --> 00:02:31.435
Long enough that the sun will die

00:02:31.435 --> 00:02:31.834
first.
